1. Introduction
ASP.NET MVC (Model-View-Controller) is a framework by Microsoft for building dynamic, testable, and scalable web applications.

It separates application logic into three main components:

Model → Business logic & data.

View → UI layer.

Controller → Handles requests, orchestrates between Model & View.

2. Key Features
Separation of Concerns → Easier maintainability.

Testability → Supports unit testing & dependency injection.

Routing → URL patterns mapped to controllers/actions.

Convention over Configuration → Reduces boilerplate.

Filters → For cross-cutting concerns (auth, logging, caching).

Strongly Typed Views → Razor syntax with IntelliSense.

State Management → TempData, ViewData, ViewBag, Session.

3. Lifecycle
Request enters via Routing.

Controller action executes.

Action interacts with Model.

Data passed to View.

Response rendered back to client.

4. Routing
Default Route: {controller}/{action}/{id}

Custom routes can be defined in RouteConfig.cs.

Attribute Routing introduced in MVC 5: [Route("products/{id}")].

5. Razor View Engine
Lightweight syntax: @Model.Name, @Html.TextBoxFor().

Supports layouts, partial views, and sections.

6. State Management
ViewData: Dictionary, weakly typed.

ViewBag: Dynamic wrapper around ViewData.

TempData: Persists across one redirect.

Session: Server-side storage.

7. Filters
Authorization Filter → Security.

Action Filter → Pre/post-processing.

Result Filter → Modify result before rendering.

Exception Filter → Handle errors globally.

8. Security
Authentication: Forms, Windows, OAuth.

Authorization: Role-based, claims-based.

Anti-Forgery Tokens: Prevent CSRF.

Data Validation: Model validation attributes.

9. Advanced Concepts
Dependency Injection → Inversion of Control (IoC).

Bundling & Minification → Optimize scripts/styles.

Areas → Modularize large applications.

Scaffolding → Auto-generate CRUD code.

Entity Framework Integration → ORM for database access.

10. Current Market Relevance
ASP.NET MVC is widely used in enterprise apps, though ASP.NET Core MVC is now the modern standard.

Companies still maintain legacy MVC apps, so knowledge of both is valuable.

Interview focus: Routing, Filters, Security, State Management, EF integration, REST APIs, Authentication.

🎯 25 Interview Questions & Answers (2 Years Experience, Market-Relevant)
Core MVC
Q: What is MVC architecture and why is it used?
A: MVC separates concerns: Model (business/data), View (UI), Controller (request handling). It improves maintainability, scalability, and testability.

Q: Difference between ASP.NET WebForms and MVC?
A: WebForms → event-driven, ViewState, tightly coupled. MVC → lightweight, no ViewState, better testability, clean separation.

Q: Explain Routing in MVC.
A: Routing maps URLs to controller actions. Default route: {controller}/{action}/{id}. Attribute routing allows custom patterns.

Q: What is Razor View Engine?
A: Razor is a markup syntax for embedding server code in HTML. Example: @Model.Name. It’s lightweight and supports IntelliSense.

Q: Difference between ViewData, ViewBag, and TempData?
A: ViewData → dictionary, weakly typed. ViewBag → dynamic wrapper. TempData → persists across redirects.

Filters & Security
Q: Types of filters in MVC?
A: Authorization, Action, Result, Exception filters.

Q: How do you implement authentication in MVC?
A: Forms authentication, Identity framework, OAuth, JWT in APIs.

Q: What is CSRF and how to prevent it?
A: Cross-Site Request Forgery. Prevent using @Html.AntiForgeryToken() and [ValidateAntiForgeryToken].

Q: How do you handle exceptions globally?
A: Using HandleErrorAttribute or custom exception filters.

Q: Explain role-based authorization.
A: [Authorize(Roles="Admin,Manager")] restricts access based on user roles.

State & Performance
Q: How do you manage state in MVC?
A: ViewData, ViewBag, TempData, Session, Cookies.

Q: What is Bundling and Minification?
A: Bundling combines multiple files; Minification removes whitespace/comments. Improves performance.

Q: How do you optimize an MVC application?
A: Caching, async controllers, bundling, minification, database indexing.

Q: Difference between Session and TempData?
A: Session persists until timeout; TempData persists only for one redirect.

Q: How do you implement caching in MVC?
A: [OutputCache(Duration=60)] caches action results.

Entity Framework & Data
Q: How do you connect MVC with a database?
A: Using Entity Framework (Code First, Database First). Define DbContext, DbSet, use LINQ queries.

Q: Difference between Lazy Loading and Eager Loading in EF?
A: Lazy → loads related data on demand. Eager → loads related data upfront using .Include().

Q: How do you perform CRUD in MVC?
A: Scaffold controllers/views with EF, or manually implement Create, Read, Update, Delete actions.

Q: What is Dependency Injection in MVC?
A: Injecting dependencies (services, repositories) via constructors. Improves testability and decoupling.

Q: How do you implement Repository Pattern?
A: Abstracts data access logic. Example: IRepository<T> with methods like GetAll(), Add(), Delete().

Advanced & Market-Relevant
Q: Difference between ASP.NET MVC and ASP.NET Core MVC?
A: Core is cross-platform, lightweight, built-in DI, unified pipeline. Classic MVC is Windows-only, heavier.

Q: How do you secure sensitive data in MVC apps?
A: Encryption, HTTPS, secure cookies, Identity framework.

Q: How do you implement REST APIs in MVC?
A: Using Web API controllers, returning JSON/XML, attribute routing.

Q: What is Middleware in ASP.NET Core?
A: Components in request pipeline (auth, logging, routing). Not in classic MVC, but relevant for migration.

Q: What are current trends in .NET development?
A: Migration to ASP.NET Core, microservices, containerization (Docker/Kubernetes), cloud integration (Azure), Identity & JWT-based auth.
